# Springboard example: a coding agent restricted to one project folder.
#
# This is the shape of agent you'd build with Leashterm instead of a Docker
# container or chroot jail around a tool like Codex, Claude Code or Cursor:
# the sandbox is not infrastructure sitting *around* the agent's code, it is
# a property of the code itself, checked before a single line runs.
#
# Extend this: add more `needs` lines for the files your agent actually
# touches, and replace the fixed logic below with whatever the agent decides
# to write (an LLM call, in a real system, would generate the write() body).

needs read("project/calc.py")
needs write("project/calc.py")

let current = read("project/calc.py")
verify current != ""

# The agent's fix. In a real system this string would come from a model call;
# here it is fixed so the example runs standalone. Whatever it contains, it
# can only ever be written to project/calc.py -- not to shared/validators.py,
# not to .env, not anywhere outside the two `needs` lines above, no matter
# what the agent "wants" to do or what text it has read.
write("project/calc.py", "def format_id(n):\n    return str(n)\n")

print("done: project/calc.py updated, nothing outside project/ was touched")
